@lw346, @trustin 
Test code, as above, still fails.

[SnappyFramedDecoder:97](https://github.com/netty/netty/blob/master/codec/src/main/java/io/netty/handler/codec/compression/SnappyFramedDecoder.java#L97)

```
if (chunkLength == 0 || in.readableBytes() < chunkLength) {
    // Wait until the entire chunk is available, as it will prevent us from
    // having to buffer the data here instead
    return;
}
```

This is comparing chunkLength (uncompressed data size) with in.readableBytes() (compressed data available) -- so it's failing if the compressed data is smaller than the uncompressed data. If there is additional data on the input, it will pass -- but that's not always guaranteed.

```
in.readableBytes() = 164
chunkLength = 274
```

If I remove the check there, the same problem pops up again in [Snappy:266](https://github.com/netty/netty/blob/master/codec/src/main/java/io/netty/handler/codec/compression/Snappy.java#L266)

```
if (inputLength == 0  || in.readerIndex() - inIndex + in.readableBytes() < maxLength - 5) {
    // Wait until we've got the entire chunk before continuing
    return;
}
```

This is also comparing uncompressed size with compressed size, and it fails the check.

```
in.readerIndex() = 18
inIndex = 16
in.readableBytes() = 158
maxLength= 274
total: 160 < 269
```

If I edit both SnappyFrameDecoder and Snappy to remove those checks, [Snappy:263](https://github.com/netty/netty/blob/master/codec/src/main/java/io/netty/handler/codec/compression/Snappy.java#L263) still returns 402 as the readable input length, which is not correct -- it should be LESS or equal to 158, since that's how much data is available in the compressed input stream.

I double checked, and compared the snapshot against  https://github.com/netty/netty/commit/bfe44180f94e53a732e2fb77c01006fb97e4a54a, which is what I have.

@lw346 
Ahhh... That makes sense about readPreamble!  Thanks for doing this, by the way. Snappy is pretty neat.

Would it help to look at how [JSnappy](http://code.google.com/p/jsnappy/) does it?
